00:14:57.760 --> 00:15:15.439
Jiaheng Li: Okay, thank you. Okay, I'm Jiaheng, I'm a master's student at ETH Zurich, and we worked on this project with Anton also at ETH Zurich, Yann and our professor. We were studying about the security of P2P networks, and specifically the liveness of gossiping protocols.

119
00:15:16.060 --> 00:15:16.820
Jiaheng Li: Yep.

120
00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:31.049
Jiaheng Li: Yeah, so P2P network serves as a communication layer for the large decentralized systems, such as Ethereum, and gossip protocols are there to spread the messages

121
00:15:31.210 --> 00:15:32.889
Jiaheng Li: BNs those systems.

122
00:15:33.020 --> 00:15:36.780
Jiaheng Li: And, for example, for the Consensus of the ethereum.

123
00:15:36.940 --> 00:15:46.890
Jiaheng Li: While the consensus mechanism itself has been extensively studied by the community, the underlying gossip layer has been far less rigorously understood.

124
00:15:46.960 --> 00:16:00.219
Jiaheng Li: And the analysis of the consensus mechanism assumes a timely delivery of the underlying gossip layer, so the security study of the underlying gossip protocols is also important.

125
00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:07.449
Jiaheng Li: So, we can model the network as a graph with nodes and links between peers.

126
00:16:07.490 --> 00:16:22.270
Jiaheng Li: And gossiping is a problem to spread the message published by some notes to the others in this graph. And the most naive method is flooding, that every node forwards everything they received to every other

127
00:16:22.310 --> 00:16:33.840
Jiaheng Li: peers, and this is robust because it utilizes every edge in the network, but it could be highly redundant, because many of the forwarding could be duplicates.

128
00:16:34.230 --> 00:16:45.150
Jiaheng Li: There are many practical optimizations to this, trying to reduce the redundancy by selectively forwarding the messages. They may select

129
00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:57.269
Jiaheng Li: which messages they may forward, or, like, which peers they forward to. And the question here is, are these optimizations really secure under an adversary scenario?

130
00:16:58.990 --> 00:17:05.640
Jiaheng Li: So one possible object… objective of the adversary could be to break the liveness of the gossiping.

131
00:17:05.780 --> 00:17:12.070
Jiaheng Li: So, if the… adversary targets one node, they may try to

132
00:17:12.069 --> 00:17:28.040
Jiaheng Li: cut all the fresh messages to this node, so that no new messages could be delivered to that. Or if they targeted the full network, they could cause a partition of the network. So… so, like, they could not proceed with the message forwarding.

133
00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:42.859
Jiaheng Li: And one adversary strategy here is to utilize the previously mentioned selection mechanism of optimization. So, because the mechanism will try to select which

134
00:17:43.210 --> 00:17:58.609
Jiaheng Li: which nodes they talk to, or which messages they forward. The adversary may manage to occupy all of these, so that the honest links will be filtered out, and all the preserved ones are controlled by the adversary, actually.

135
00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:02.230
Jiaheng Li: And we want to study whether this could happen.

136
00:18:04.140 --> 00:18:14.199
Jiaheng Li: So, we study two concrete gossiping protocols, DOC, which is designed to be used for common BFT, and also gossip-sub, which is used by Ethereum.

137
00:18:14.360 --> 00:18:21.320
Jiaheng Li: For Doc, we identified a pretty practical attack with only

138
00:18:21.380 --> 00:18:37.990
Jiaheng Li: one physical node required, running a few instances, and the adversary result could be all the forwarding routes being disabled, which means no one in this network is forwarding any messages. So message could only reach their direct neighbors.

139
00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:52.900
Jiaheng Li: And in addition, we find the DAC protocol itself is not, like, safe in theory, that we find a counterexample, even without an adversary, that the connectivity may not be secured.

140
00:18:53.260 --> 00:19:09.539
Jiaheng Li: And for gossip sub, it is more robust, and we assume a geometrical model for the latency, and also some other assumptions. And under these assumptions, it may still be possible for the adversary to cause

141
00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:15.880
Jiaheng Li: To cause a separation of the constant fraction of the network with a constant number of

142
00:19:15.880 --> 00:19:27.370
Jiaheng Li: adversary nodes, plus the advantage on latency, targeting the main mechanism. Well, for the additional mechanisms, like the off-bound quota, it requires some additional

143
00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:31.880
Jiaheng Li: requirements, and I have… I want mechanism is not considered here.

144
00:19:32.970 --> 00:19:36.340
Jiaheng Li: So let's move to the technical part of the doc.

145
00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:45.169
Jiaheng Li: So, reminds the goal of the gossiping algorithm is to try to reduce redundancy in this forwarding, and, like.

146
00:19:45.320 --> 00:19:53.889
Jiaheng Li: Ideally, one potential goal could be to get something like a spending tree, because that is the most efficient way to get connectivity.

147
00:19:54.270 --> 00:20:06.140
Jiaheng Li: And the… the method of doc to achieve this is root filtering. So it… when it finds one root is duplicate, it will filter it out, and that root will not be used again.

148
00:20:06.600 --> 00:20:23.999
Jiaheng Li: So, a root here is a basic forwarding unit for an intermediate node U. A root is a pair from P2 to T that U will forward messages it received from P2 to another neighbor T.

149
00:20:25.140 --> 00:20:26.100
Jiaheng Li: And…

150
00:20:26.480 --> 00:20:40.550
Jiaheng Li: And if T finds this route to be duplicate, if T receives a duplicate method from U2, and it will ask U2 to filter this route out, so next time, U2 will not forward this.

151
00:20:40.660 --> 00:20:50.419
Jiaheng Li: forward via this redundant routes. So, for example, if S1 published some message here, and it could be propagated like this.

152
00:20:50.560 --> 00:21:05.310
Jiaheng Li: And… but also, U2 will also try to propagate this message from… it's received from P2 to T, but this one is duplicate, and T will find this to be duplicate, and this root will be filtered out by U2.

153
00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:10.429
Jiaheng Li: So next… so next time, YouTube will not forward messages from P2 to T.

154
00:21:11.160 --> 00:21:17.839
Jiaheng Li: if… If someone publishes some other message, this… this duplicate message will not be there again.

155
00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:30.910
Jiaheng Li: And there is also a redundancy control mechanism, which allows some duplicates for robustness, and it will adjust the… the filtering to make the…

156
00:21:31.180 --> 00:21:38.170
Jiaheng Li: A redundancy level between a range, but we see this mechanism does not make much difference here.

157
00:21:40.310 --> 00:21:41.260
Jiaheng Li: And…

158
00:21:41.450 --> 00:21:56.809
Jiaheng Li: We study from the adversary's perspective, and we find an adversary construction that the adversary may be able to filter out any route in the network. For example, if the adversary targets a route F to P to T, and they

159
00:21:56.920 --> 00:22:15.110
Jiaheng Li: themselves could also control, like, one node in the network. They could publish one message M itself, and they can deliberately control the latency to S and to T to make sure that this message first reached T directly, and then…

160
00:22:15.310 --> 00:22:33.440
Jiaheng Li: that it can also reach T where this route asks P to T later. And in this case, this route will be redundant, and it will be filtered out. And this could be achieved by the adversary deliberately control the latencies and publish its own message.

161
00:22:34.760 --> 00:22:40.540
Jiaheng Li: And the redundancy control mechanism could also be addressed with some additional messages involved here.

162
00:22:41.470 --> 00:22:44.500
Jiaheng Li: So, if the adversary repeats this.

163
00:22:44.900 --> 00:22:51.080
Jiaheng Li: this attack per route for… for one target node. It could filter out all the…

164
00:22:51.740 --> 00:23:08.399
Jiaheng Li: forwarding those to that target, and then that target will be separated, that no one will forward any messages to it. And they could even repeat this per node throughout the network, and the result is the full network will be disconnected, that no one will forward any messages.

165
00:23:10.080 --> 00:23:18.230
Jiaheng Li: And also, while the theoretical aim of the… of dog is…

166
00:23:18.230 --> 00:23:33.159
Jiaheng Li: of the root filtering of stock is to get something like a spanning tree, but we find this is not really the case in theory, and we also identify a small counterexample even without adversary notes. So, in this

167
00:23:33.160 --> 00:23:44.029
Jiaheng Li: RANDA 5 nodes. If M… if they will publish a method M1, it could be propagated like this, and as a result, these two routes will be redundant and filtered out.

168
00:23:44.590 --> 00:23:48.590
Jiaheng Li: The same could happen again if one published some messages.

169
00:23:48.690 --> 00:23:51.670
Jiaheng Li: And so far, it's still working, but…

170
00:23:51.830 --> 00:24:00.350
Jiaheng Li: If we put it together, we could see all the four routes are filtered out. And if node 2 publish another method now.

171
00:24:00.350 --> 00:24:14.810
Jiaheng Li: Nether of 0 and 1 will forward this message, because all of the two routes are filtered out, and basically the network is not connected at this time, even without an active adversary here.

172
00:24:17.400 --> 00:24:23.839
Jiaheng Li: So, to summarize for Doc, we identified an adversary attack that

